{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/600697-ancient-morality-and-the-modern-age","id":600697,"title":"Ancient morality and the modern age.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-07-23T10:28:58-07:00","id":600697,"title":"Ancient morality and the modern age.","content":"<b>At Ajax's suggestion I'll move this here and open it up to others who might wish to discuss other aspects of various cultures or how to implement such ancient/modern tensions in their games if they wish to do so.</b><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Was Loki being a mare used against him in the flytting? He definitely had no issue using every event in Norse mythology against them. Flytting are two way streets, insult contests. (To which the Aesir were pretty poor losers since they were supposed to offer Loki a drink for beating them). </div> </div> </div>Yes it was: Nj\u00f6r\u00f0r:&quot;It is no great wonder, if silk-clad dames get themselves husbands, lovers; but 'tis a wonder that a wretched man, that has borne children, should herein enter.&quot;<br /> <br /> Also, your misinterpreting the happenings of Lokasenna. A flyting is supposed to be poetic and goodnatured, something done frequently for entertainment among friends. Loki is not poetic he is merely insulting, falling down drunk and referring to insults that are inappropriate for a flyting being the very worse insults imaginable to the norse. They are supposed to be comical and grandiose rather than malicious. That's why the gods get so angry.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Was screwing animals cited as a specific offense in legal codes? Germanic and Norse people were ridiculously litigious. If they had a problem with it, they would have a law against it, a prescribed punishment and a fee you could pay to get out of it. </div> </div> </div>Yes, though the only actual lawcode in Iceland was a little after conversion, it doesn't present anything dramatically different and their was still norse pagans around at that time. Another instance preconversion was when a man named Brand invented the Mock Court and called it the &quot;The laws of the Dirty Women&quot;. Understandably the women eventually stopped going as its heavily implied that the court events were based around various sexual perversions such as zoophilia and adultery, which were described in detail by &quot;Witnesses&quot;. That of course could not have happened if sex with animals was not considered both shameful and illegal.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Norse weren't as self-effacing and face based as your are portraying them. </div> </div> </div>They weren't self-effacing at all, braggery was (and is) a religious ritual named after Bragi. Humility as a virtue is a christian addition.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Being a canny individualist who could take advantage of a situation was very well-thought of. And it wasn't to the just make one's family more prestigious. Self-aggrandizement and individual initiative was prized. Coming from a bilateral non-lineage kinship based on difficult acquisition of resources (common to Inuit and modern Western Culture), it is common to see cultures with strong lineage and clan collectivization mechanisms as being face based, but, they . ertainly don't have to be. People may be accountable to family, but family is not their measure. Lots of sagas about setting out and doing better on your own for yourself. And you get stories about that. Which means it's considered laudable. </div> </div> </div>There was really no difference between personal and family glory. the concept of the Hamingja was such that each man had a &quot;Luck&quot; or soul derived from the clan and family they belonged to which took in the personal glory and deeds of each individual to strengthen the greater unit. It also limited the actions and behavior of its members, for instance, The family of a king was the only one that was able to lead a tribe/nation because everyone else lacked that particular facet of their Luck.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Self-aggrandizement and individual initiative was prized. Coming from a bilateral non-lineage kinship based on difficult acquisition of resources (common to Inuit and modern Western Culture), it is common to see cultures with strong lineage and clan collectivization mechanisms as being face based, but, they . ertainly don't have to be. People may be accountable to family, but family is not their measure. Lots of sagas about setting out and doing better on your own for yourself. And you get stories about that. Which means it's considered laudable. Also, even having bragging and flytting means you aren't looking at a culture where honor is specifically tied to a lineage based self-identity </div> </div> </div>I'd recommend reading Wilhelm Gronbech's &quot;Culture of the Tuetons&quot; (I won't hold it against you if you don't, its very academic and long) where he goes in depth into the Germanic concept of honor (which is markedly different from the modern concept) and how it was invariably tied to ones birth and station in life. (The germanic people had an actual caste system). I would transcribe some here but he has a long winded writing style and its better to simply read it yourself.<br /> <br /> Honour, the soul of the clan: <a href=\"http://www.northvegr.org/secondary%20sources/religion%20culture%20history/the%20culture%20of%20the%20teutons/015.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.northvegr.org/secondary%2...utons/015.html</a><br /> <br /> The soul of the man is the soul of the clan: <a href=\"http://www.northvegr.org/secondary%20sources/religion%20culture%20history/the%20culture%20of%20the%20teutons/038.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.northvegr.org/secondary%2...utons/038.html</a><br /> <br /> Birth: <a href=\"http://www.northvegr.org/secondary%20sources/religion%20culture%20history/the%20culture%20of%20the%20teutons/040.html\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.northvegr.org/secondary%2...utons/040.html</a><br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And, no, it's still not Cthulhu-esque. It's obviously not unthinkable if people think badly of it. It's ENTIRELY thinkable. We can think about cannibalism and consider it awful Same with slavery and incest. People who do it are bad people. If you think people who do it are bad people, you've thought about it. None of that is Cthulhu-esque. It's not like the Norse would see said act and their minds would not be able to process it. It wouldn't be like suddenly there was a 5th lateral direction or an explosion of a new color that no one had ever seen before or both man and horse turned inside out, conjoin and then sprouted pustule/eyes and tentacles or simply that seeing such a thing would reveal to them that the world was so much less than they thought it was and they were less than nothing with consciousness just a delusion of dancing motes in the eyes of, not the unknown, but the unknowable. They would see it and have a very specific set of thoughts, like, say, &quot;Better kill that guy for doing something that is just wrong.&quot; </div> </div> </div>Alright, i'll admit that its perhaps not the best to compare it to Cthulhu, but I trust you understand what I was getting at?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3835-\u00falfh\u00e9\u00f0nar","name":"\u00dalfh\u00e9\u00f0nar","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"108"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}